// RED TEAM assault round 1, non-capability lens (2026-08-07).
//
// A SIXTH unguarded recursive-descent parse cycle, distinct from the
// already-filed `attack_parser_elif_let_chain_stack_overflow.kry` (item:
// statement-context `if let ... elif let` desugar chain via
// parse_if_rest/parse_if_let/parse_if_let_else). This one is the
// EXPRESSION-context sibling: an `if ... elif ... elif ... else ...`
// chain used as a VALUE (e.g. the initializer of a `let`, a match-arm
// tail, a function argument) is parsed by a completely separate pair of
// functions, `parse_if_expr` / `parse_if_expr_after_if`
// (compiler/crates/kryos-parser/src/parser.rs ~2905-2989), reached via
// `parse_primary`'s `TokenKind::If => self.parse_if_expr()` arm
// (~line 2633) -- NOT through `parse_if_stmt`/`parse_if_rest` at all, so
// fixing the statement-level bug does not fix this one.
//
// Read directly: neither `parse_if_expr` nor `parse_if_expr_after_if`
// calls `self.nesting_exhausted()` anywhere, and the `elif` arm of each
// (`self.advance(); let nested_if = self.parse_if_expr_after_if(start)`)
// and the `else if` arm (`let nested_if = self.parse_if_expr()`) are both
// direct, unbounded native Rust recursion -- the same shape as the
// already-fixed-guard-missing statement case, in a sibling code path the
// fix for that case will NOT cover.
//
// This 3-clause file is the harmless control: parses clean, reaches the
// checker, exits 1 with an ordinary diagnostic (undefined var `x`/`b`/`c`
// deliberately unbound, or just a type mismatch) -- proving the
// CONSTRUCT itself is accepted at small depth and the crash is a scale
// effect, not a rejection of the syntax:
fn main() {
    let a: bool = true
    let b: bool = false
    let c: bool = false
    let x: i64 = if a { 1 } elif b { 2 } elif c { 3 } else { 0 }
    println(to_string(x))
}

// At scale (generated, not committed at full size -- mirrors the sibling
